## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compact, eviction-aware data structure for time-windowed counting, a common pattern in real-time analytics.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window eviction via timestamped events
- Bucketed counts: map from count to set of users for fast top-k
- Invariants: update counts atomically during insertion and eviction
- Complexity: O(log n) updates, O(k log n) top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.count = new Map(); // user -> cnt
    this.buckets = new Map(); // cnt -> Set<user>
    this.events = new Deque(); // (ts, user)
  }
  _addToBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) this.buckets.set(cnt, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(cnt).add(user);
  }
  _removeFromBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) return;
    this.buckets.get(cnt).delete(user);
    if (this.buckets.get(cnt).size === 0) this.buckets.delete(cnt);
  }
  prune(now) {
    while (this.events.length && this.events.peek().ts <= now - this.W) {
      const {ts, user} = this.events.shift();
      const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
      if (c > 0) {
        this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
        const nc = c - 1;
        if (nc > 0) {
          this._addToBucket(nc, user);
          this.count.set(user, nc);
        } else {
          this.count.delete(user);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  addEvent(user, ts) {
    this.events.push({ts, user});
    this.prune(ts);
    const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
    if (c > 0) this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
    const nc = c + 1;
    this._addToBucket(nc, user);
    this.count.set(user, nc);
  }
  topK(now, k) {
    const res = [];
    // iterate counts from high to low
    const counts = Array.from(this.buckets.keys()).sort((a,b)=>b-a);
    for (const cnt of counts) {
      const set = this.buckets.get(cnt);
      const users = Array.from(set).sort((a,b)=>a-b); // tie-breaker by userId
      for (const u of users) {
        res.push([u, cnt]);
        if (res.length === k) return res;
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support multiple simultaneous windows (e.g., W1 and W2) efficiently?
- How would you handle very large userId spaces with memory constraints, or concurrent updates in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic connectivity with insertions and deletions is a classic hard problem. This offline approach tests understanding of edge lifetimes and versioning, and how to combine segment trees with DSU rollback for log-scale updates.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with rollback (no path compression) to enable undo.
- Segment tree over time to map edge active intervals to time ranges.
- Offline processing: traverse tree, apply unions, answer leaf queries, then rollback.

## Code Example
```javascript
class RollbackDSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.sz = Array(n).fill(1);
    this.ch = [];
  }
  find(x){ while (this.parent[x]!==x) x = this.parent[x]; return x; }
  union(a,b){
    a = this.find(a); b = this.find(b);
    if (a===b){ this.ch.push(null); return; }
    if (this.sz[a] < this.sz[b]) [a,b] = [b,a];
    this.ch.push([b, this.parent[b], a, this.sz[a]]);
    this.parent[b] = a; this.sz[a] += this.sz[b];
  }
  snapshot(){ return this.ch.length; }
  rollback(snap){
    while (this.ch.length > snap){
      const ch = this.ch.pop();
      if (!ch) continue;
      const [b, oldP, a, oldS] = ch;
      this.parent[b] = oldP; this.sz[a] = oldS;
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests a practical DS for dynamic strings with fast prefix queries and duplicates, common in user-name suggestions.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node counters for duplicates
- endCount vs. subCount to distinguish completions from prefixes
- O(length) per operation for add/remove/prefixCount

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor() { this.next = new Map(); this.subCount = 0; this.endCount = 0; } }
class Trie { constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  addUser(name){ let node = this.root; for (const ch of name){ if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode()); node = node.next.get(ch); node.subCount++; } node.endCount++; }
  prefixCount(prefix){ let node = this.root; for (const ch of prefix){ if (!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.subCount; }
  removeUser(name){ let stack = [this.root]; let node = this.root; for (const ch of name){ if (!node.next.has(ch)) return; stack.push(node.next.get(ch)); node = node.next.get(ch); }
    if (node.endCount > 0){ node.endCount--; for (let i = name.length - 1; i >= 0; i--){ const c = name[i]; const parent = stack[i]; const child = parent.next.get(c); child.subCount--; if (child.subCount === 0) parent.next.delete(c); } }
  }
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle memory reuse for many unique prefixes?
- How would you adapt for case-insensitive matching or non-ASCII usernames?

